# Declarations

A declaration is written as a function is, up to the result type, and
stops there. This page reads the parts of a declaration that a function
with a body does not have, each from a line of the standard library.

## The receiver

A first parameter named `self` is the **receiver**: the value a call is
made on. A declaration with a receiver is reached by a receiver call,
`value.f()`, by a pipe, `value | f`, and by an ordinary call with the
receiver first:

```hql
[abs(-3), (-3).abs(), -3 | abs]
```

```text
[3, 3, 3]
```

A declaration without `self` takes its first argument the same way, as the
value the call is made on, and is not reached by a receiver call.

## Positional and named parameters

A `/` in a parameter list ends the parameters a call writes by position
only, and a `*` begins the ones it writes by name only. `pow` is declared
`fn pow(self: Int, exponent: Int, /) : Int`, so its exponent has no name
at a call:

```hql
pow(2, 10)
```

```text
1024
```

```hql
pow(2, exponent = 10)
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `pow` takes `exponent` by position: write the value without `exponent =`
 --> <expression>:1:19
  |
1 | pow(2, exponent = 10)
  |                   ^^
```

A parameter after `*` is the reverse: `fn shuffle<T>(self: Seq<T>, *, seed:
Int) : List<T>` takes `seed` by name, `xs.shuffle(seed = 7)`, and never by
position. `/` and `*` are written in declarations only; a function with a
body takes its arguments by position.

## Defaults

A parameter with `= value` after its type may be left out of a call. The
declaration states the default so that a reader sees it; the Rust function
supplies it, since an argument a call does not write is absent when the
function runs. [[34-writing-an-extension/natives|Natives]] shows one.

## Forms of one name

A name may be declared more than once, each line a **form**. A call takes
the first form its receiver and arguments fit:

```text
fn abs(self: Int) : Int
fn abs(self: Float) : Float
fn abs(self: Decimal) : Decimal
fn abs(self: Duration) : Duration
```

```hql
[abs(-3), abs(-1.5f).int(), abs(-2.50).int()]
```

```text
[3, 1, 2]
```

The forms of a name share one Rust function, which reads the value it was
given. A function with a body has one form.

## Purity

A declaration written `fn` is **pure**: its result depends on its
arguments alone, so the planner may reorder it, run it once for equal
arguments, or skip it when nothing reads the result. A declaration written
`effectful fn` runs once, where it is written. The two forms of `shuffle`
differ in exactly this:

```text
fn shuffle<T>(self: Seq<T>, *, seed: Int) : List<T>
effectful fn shuffle<T>(self: Seq<T>) : List<T>
```

With a seed the result is a function of the seed and the list. Without one
the call draws from the evaluation's seed and is effectful.

## Shape

A word before `fn` states how a backend may split the input of a call:

| Word | What it states |
| --- | --- |
| `elementwise fn` | the result for each element depends on that element alone |
| `partition fn` | the input is regrouped by a key, and the groups complete in any order |
| `reduction fn` | the whole input collapses to one value through a combiner |
| none | a **barrier**: the whole input is needed before any output |

`count` is declared `reduction fn count<T>(self: Collection<T>) : Int`: a
count of a collection is the sum of the counts of its parts.

```hql
[1, 2, 3].count()
```

```text
3
```

Purity and shape are claims about the Rust function. The declaration
states them, the extension marks its function the same way, and the two
markings are compared when the package is bound
([[how-std-is-built|next page]]).
